    "elevatorPitch": "Anger is not a character flaw or a beast to cage; it is a sensitive dashboard warning light signaling an internal emergency. Discover how to treat emotional flare-ups like kitchen grease fires\u2014cooling the heat instantly with breath and compost rather than fueling the blaze.",
    "executiveSummary": "When anger ignites, our baseline nervous system triggers an ancient fight-or-flight feedback loop. Most modern advice tells people either to vent their fury into a pillow or lock it behind iron willpower. Both approaches fail. Venting acts like blowing air into an open hearth\u2014it rehearses and strengthens neural pathways of aggression. Suppressing it acts like sealing a pressure cooker without a safety valve until the gasket fails. Thich Nhat Hanh presents a third, systems-level approach: recognize anger as an organic symptom of unaddressed pain and misperception.\n\nThink of your emotional body like a productive garden. Rotting vegetable scraps and weeds smell offensive, yet no experienced gardener curses the compost. Instead, the gardener folds the organic waste into rich soil, using warmth and time to grow crisp greens and fragrant blossoms. In the exact same way, anger, jealousy, and resentment are raw compost. When held in the steady light of mindful awareness, they transform into actionable empathy and clarity.\n\nInterpersonally, anger always operates inside closed communication loops. When someone stabs us with harsh words, our instinct is to retaliate immediately. Yet when your own house catches fire, running after the suspected arsonist while your living room burns to the ground is fatal. The only rational engineering move is to secure your home first. By taking ownership of your emotional reactor, calming your physiological storm, and initiating structured communication, you break inherited habit loops and restore systemic balance.",
