      "headline": "You Are What You Fuel",
      "keyInsight": "We often treat anger like an unwelcome burglar breaking in through the window. But look closer at the kitchen pantry. You bought the ingredients yourself. Every violent show, nasty argument, and bitter article you consume enters your system directly. Your body cooks whatever you feed it. When you swallow toxic junk, your mind brews a burning stew. You cannot eat rotten food and expect a calm stomach. You cannot feed on aggression and expect a tranquil heart. The real twist is that anger is not born out of thin air. It is literally an indigestion problem of the mind. Change the daily diet, and the fire runs out of wood.",
      "content": "Think of your mind as an organic kitchen garden. Whatever seeds you water will grow into full plants. When you read venomous comments online or watch screaming matches on television, you pour salty water over thorn bushes. Soon enough, sharp weeds take over the entire yard. We think we are just passing time, but our nervous system absorbs every single drop.\n\nFood also plays a massive role in this hidden plumbing. When an animal lives in terror and stress, that panic gets locked into its meat. When you cook it, you absorb that chemical distress directly into your blood. Heavy, grease-laden meals strain your digestion, turning your body into an exhausted pressure cooker. An irritated gut makes an irritated temper. The connection between stomach and temper is absolute and direct.\n\nTake a close look at your daily consumption habits. Are you sipping fresh water or swallowing gasoline? You cannot stop anger by fighting it with more anger. You stop it by cutting off the fuel supply. Clear out the toxic inputs from your diet, your screens, and your conversations. When the fire has no wood to chew on, it naturally dies down into gentle ash.",
