      "headline": "Putting Out the House Fire",
      "keyInsight": "When your wooden house catches fire, what is your first move? Do you sprint down the street to tackle the suspect who threw the match? Of course not. Chasing the arsonist while your living room burns to the ground is complete madness. You grab a water hose and save your own home first. Yet when someone insults us, we do the exact opposite. We abandon our burning heart and spend all our energy shouting at the other person. The secret is counterintuitive: stop chasing the villain. Turn around, enter your smoking house, and cradle your burning feelings like a warm blanket. Self-rescue comes before any debate.",
      "content": "Picture your anger as a crying, screaming infant who woke up in sudden pain. If a baby starts wailing, a loving mother does not punch the wall or yell back. She stops her cooking, walks over gently, picks up the baby, and rocks it in her arms. Your anger is your own flesh and blood. It is an injured part of you asking for immediate first aid.\n\nMost people try venting anger by punching pillows or screaming into gym mats. But science and nature show that rehearsing rage only builds bigger highways for rage in the brain. When you smash a cushion, you are not releasing the beast; you are training it to bite harder next time. You are practicing the exact rhythm of destruction.\n\nInstead, use conscious breathing as a cool wet towel. Inhale deeply and say to yourself, \"I know I am angry.\" Exhale slowly and say, \"I am caring for my anger.\" You do not suppress the fire, and you do not fuel it either. You embrace it with pure awareness until the flame cools into clear light.",
