      "chapterNumber": 7,
      "headline": "No Enemies: Dismantling the Target Board",
      "keyInsight": "We love having an enemy. An enemy gives our fury a neat address and a bullseye. If your partner snaps at you, you want to fire back. But look closer under the hood. The person hurting you is drowning in their own misery. When someone is on fire, they wildly swing their arms and burn whatever is nearby. Blaming them is like suing a burning tree for scorching your fence. The real adversary is not the human standing in front of you. The true enemy is the unmanaged pain driving their behavior. Punishing them only pours gasoline on the blaze. If you help extinguish their flames instead, your own home stops catching sparks.",
      "content": "Imagine you are driving down a narrow mountain road at dusk. Another car suddenly swerves straight into your lane and cuts you off. Your hands grip the wheel tight. Your heart races, and you feel ready to honk and yell. But then you glance inside their window. The driver is slumped over, clutching their chest in agony. Instantly, your wrath evaporates into concern. The sudden shift did not happen because their bad driving improved. It shifted because your view of the situation changed.\n\nMost conflict operates on this exact hidden loop. When people lash out, we assume they are acting with cold malice. We imagine an evil mastermind plotting our downfall. In reality, bad behavior is almost always a clumsy leak of inner distress. People rarely hurt others out of strength. They hurt others because their own internal pressure cooker lacks a safety valve. When you see someone's secret pain, you lose your target.\n\nThink of a kitchen fire. If a pot of oil catches fire on the stove, you do not beat the pan with a stick. You slide a lid over it to cut off the oxygen. Treating difficult people as targets only fans the flames. The moment you stop hunting for a villain, you reclaim all your energy. You shift from a fighter trapped in a feud to a firefighter restoring calm.",
