      "headline": "David and Angelina: The Habit Energy of Anger",
      "keyInsight": "Ever wonder why you blow up over small things like dirty socks or late dinners? You think you are reacting to the moment, but you are actually running ancient software. Thich Nhat Hanh calls this habit energy. Just like an old wagon wheel slips naturally into worn-out dirt ruts, your brain defaults to old patterns copied straight from your parents. David and Angelina love each other, yet they keep stabbing each other with sharp words. Neither wants to fight, but the track is so deeply carved that autopilot takes the wheel before reason wakes up. The trap is believing you chose the reaction. You did not choose it; an unexamined mental reflex made the choice for you.",
      "content": "Picture an old record player sitting in a sunny living room. The needle hits a deep scratch on the vinyl. Over and over, the same jarring beat skips and plays on repeat. Relationships get trapped in identical grooves. You swear you will stay calm during the next disagreement. Yet two sentences in, your voice spikes and sharp phrases fly out. You find yourself repeating the exact arguments your parents used thirty years ago.\n\nThis is habit energy in action. It is momentum built through decades of repetition. Your nervous system loves shortcuts to save energy. When tension spikes, your brain does not draft a thoughtful essay. It grabs the loudest, oldest script off the shelf. That script was often handed down through generations of stressed ancestors. Without clear awareness, you become a puppet moved by antique strings.\n\nBreaking a groove requires catching the wheel before it slips into the mud. You cannot rewrite an automated reflex while speeding at ninety miles per hour. You have to tap the brakes the second you feel your jaw tighten. Acknowledging the old script disarms its power. Once you spot the groove, you can finally steer onto a fresh, smooth road.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Your worst reactions are rarely spontaneous; they are old family scripts running on autopilot.",
      "audioSnippetText": "Angry blowups are often just worn-out grooves passed down through generations. Recognizing the script the moment it starts is the only way to stop the rerun.",
